# graph-traversal.zd - depth-first and breadth-first over a declared graph.
#
# The first example in this repository that computes something whose answer
# is not obvious from reading it. Every other example demonstrates a
# construct; this one runs an algorithm, and the two traversals visit the
# same eight nodes in two different orders because the frontier is a stack
# in one and a queue in the other.
#
# WHAT THE LANGUAGE MADE HARD, AND HOW THIS FILE ANSWERS IT.
#
#   1. The visited set is a `Map of Whole to Truth`, and it says what it
#      means: the set answers *whether*, and `order` stays a
#      `List of Whole` beside it because the page shows *in what order*.
#      Asking one question of one structure is the whole of why the map
#      is here, and the cost is worth being exact about, because it is
#      not the reason.
#
#      #115 and #117 gave a `Map` a construction form, and that was not
#      enough on its own: `set k to v in m` copied the whole table, so a
#      visited set traded an O(v) lookup for an O(v) insert and bought
#      nothing. #233 made the write a link in a chain that is flattened
#      on the first read — and this walk reads between every pair of
#      writes. It asks `walk.seen contains node`, which flattens; the
#      next write links onto a map that is now real, so the read after
#      *that* copies it entire. One copy per step, which is what the
#      copying insert cost. Measured on the emitted helpers, the
#      read-between-writes shape writes the same 500,500 entries at a
#      thousand keys before #233 and after it.
#
#      So this walk is still O(v * (v + e)) and not O(v + e), and the
#      switch bought legibility rather than time. What would buy the time
#      is a map with no flatten in it — a HAMT — which #233 names as its
#      own fallback and did not land. The lookup here is O(1) only
#      against a map that has already been read; against the one this
#      walk writes each step it is O(v), the same as the `order contains
#      node` list scan it replaced.
#
#      `neighboursOf` is still a scan of every edge, O(e) per node rather
#      than O(1), and it is the other half of the bound above. That
#      is an adjacency table this file does not build rather than one the
#      language cannot build — `mapOf of (entries of pairs)` works — and
#      it is left as a scan because the graph is written as a list of
#      edges and reading it that way is what makes the source legible at
#      eight nodes.
#
#   2. A queue needs to drop from the front and a stack from the back.
#      `rest of` and `listTake` do both, and both copy, so each step of the
#      breadth-first walk copies the queue. `listDrop`/`listTake` are the
#      only spelling available: there is no deque and no O(1) dequeue.
#
#   3. `from` and `to` are keywords, so an edge's ends cannot be called
#      that. They are `tail` and `head`, which is the graph theorist's
#      spelling and reads fine, but the refusal is worth knowing about
#      before you name a record field. The refusal has not changed and
#      should not: the message has. #199 is closed, and the error now
#      says what `from` *does* — introduces a pipeline's source — rather
#      than only that it is spoken for.
#
# WHAT IT DID NOT NEED. No visitor callback, no comparator, no closure.
# Every loop below is a function whose last act is to call itself with a
# longer answer, which is the shape `prelude/list.zd` opens by describing,
# and the emitter turns that call into a jump.

record Edge
    tail is Whole
    head is Whole

# An undirected graph on eight nodes, written once. Each edge appears in
# one direction and `otherEnd` reads it in whichever direction it is
# approached from, so the file does not have to list twenty edges to mean
# ten. A collection literal may not span lines, so this is one long one.
state graph is client List of Edge starting [(Edge with tail is 0, head is 1), (Edge with tail is 0, head is 2), (Edge with tail is 1, head is 3), (Edge with tail is 1, head is 4), (Edge with tail is 2, head is 5), (Edge with tail is 2, head is 6), (Edge with tail is 3, head is 7), (Edge with tail is 4, head is 7), (Edge with tail is 5, head is 7), (Edge with tail is 6, head is 7)]

state nodes is client List of Whole from range with start is 0, stop is 8

# How many nodes the stepper has been asked to visit. The one signal the
# user moves; both walks below are derived from it, so one click recomputes
# two traversals and the view swaps only the rows that changed.
state visits is client Whole starting 0

# A walk in progress: what has been visited, in order, and what is waiting.
#
# `done` is carried rather than derived because "the frontier is empty" and
# "the walk finished" are the same condition only after the first step, and
# a walk that has not started yet has a frontier of one.
#
# `seen` holds the same nodes `order` does and answers a different question
# about them: membership, in O(1), where a scan of `order` is O(v). The
# value is `yes` at every key and is never read — a `Map` is the set this
# language does not otherwise have.
record Walk
    order    is List of Whole
    frontier is List of Whole
    seen     is Map of Whole to Truth
    done     is Truth

# --- the graph -----------------------------------------------------------

# Whichever end of `edge` is not `node`. An edge touching `node` at both
# ends is a self loop and gives `node` back, which the traversal then
# skips as already seen.
function otherEnd with edge, node
    if edge.tail is node
        give edge.head
    give edge.tail

# The nodes adjacent to `node`, in the order their edges are declared.
#
# COST. O(e), where an adjacency `Map` built once would be O(degree). The
# graph is declared as a list of edges and this reads it as one; building
# the table is the change this file has not made, and it is now a choice
# rather than a blocker.
function neighboursOf with edges, node
    from edges
    keep each edge where (edge.tail is node) or (edge.head is node)
    map each edge to (otherEnd with edge is edge, node is node)

# `before` then `after`, as one list. The only concatenation the language
# has: `append` adds one element, and `flatten` of a two element literal
# adds all of them.
function followedBy with before, after
    give flatten of [before, after]

# --- depth first ---------------------------------------------------------

# One visit, popping from the back.
#
# It pops until it reaches a node it has not seen, so one call is one
# visit and the stepper always shows progress. That inner retry is the
# self call at the end of the `if`, and it is in tail position, so a run
# of already-seen nodes costs iterations and not stack.
#
# The neighbours are pushed reversed, so the first-declared neighbour is
# the one popped next. Without the `reverse of` the walk would still be a
# depth-first walk, just of the edges read backwards.
function depthStep with edges, walk
    when last of walk.frontier
        None
            give Walk with order is walk.order, frontier is empty, seen is walk.seen, done is yes
        Some with node
            with popped is (listTake with items is walk.frontier, count is (length of walk.frontier) - 1)
            if walk.seen contains node
                give depthStep with edges is edges, walk is (Walk with order is walk.order, frontier is popped, seen is walk.seen, done is no)
            give Walk with order is (append node to walk.order), frontier is (followedBy with before is popped, after is (reverse of (neighboursOf with edges is edges, node is node))), seen is (set node to yes in walk.seen), done is no

function depthFirst with edges, start, visits
    give walkOn with edges is edges, walk is (Walk with order is empty, frontier is (append start to empty), seen is empty, done is no), remaining is visits, breadth is no

# --- breadth first -------------------------------------------------------

# The same step, taking from the front instead. That single difference is
# the whole of the difference between the two algorithms, which is easier
# to see here than in a language where one uses a `Stack` class and the
# other a `Queue`.
function breadthStep with edges, walk
    when first of walk.frontier
        None
            give Walk with order is walk.order, frontier is empty, seen is walk.seen, done is yes
        Some with node
            with waiting is (rest of walk.frontier)
            if walk.seen contains node
                give breadthStep with edges is edges, walk is (Walk with order is walk.order, frontier is waiting, seen is walk.seen, done is no)
            give Walk with order is (append node to walk.order), frontier is (followedBy with before is waiting, after is (neighboursOf with edges is edges, node is node)), seen is (set node to yes in walk.seen), done is no

function breadthFirst with edges, start, visits
    give walkOn with edges is edges, walk is (Walk with order is empty, frontier is (append start to empty), seen is empty, done is no), remaining is visits, breadth is yes

# --- the driver ----------------------------------------------------------

# `remaining` visits of whichever walk `breadth` names.
#
# The `breadth` flag is where the absence of first-class functions shows
# (§17.2.5). In a language with them this would take the step function; it
# cannot, so it takes a `Truth` and branches. Two nearly identical drivers
# was the alternative and this is the smaller lie.
function walkOn with edges, walk, remaining, breadth
    if remaining < 1
        give walk
    if walk.done
        give walk
    if breadth
        give walkOn with edges is edges, walk is (breadthStep with edges is edges, walk is walk), remaining is remaining - 1, breadth is breadth
    give walkOn with edges is edges, walk is (depthStep with edges is edges, walk is walk), remaining is remaining - 1, breadth is breadth

# --- what the page shows -------------------------------------------------

state depth   is client Walk from depthFirst   with edges is graph, start is 0, visits is visits
state breadth is client Walk from breadthFirst with edges is graph, start is 0, visits is visits

# The finished walks, so the page can show the answer next to the progress.
# Eight is the node count; a walk that runs out of frontier stops early on
# its own, so the number is a bound rather than a promise.
state depthWhole   is client Walk from depthFirst   with edges is graph, start is 0, visits is 8
state breadthWhole is client Walk from breadthFirst with edges is graph, start is 0, visits is 8

function shown of numbers
    from numbers
    map each number to (text of number)

function asRow of numbers
    give join with parts is (shown of numbers), using is " "

state depthOrder   is client Text from asRow of depth.order
state breadthOrder is client Text from asRow of breadth.order
state depthQueue   is client Text from asRow of depth.frontier
state breadthQueue is client Text from asRow of breadth.frontier
state depthAnswer   is client Text from asRow of depthWhole.order
state breadthAnswer is client Text from asRow of breadthWhole.order

view
    Column
        Heading "Two walks over one graph"
        Text "Eight nodes, ten edges, both walks starting at 0. Step them together and watch the orders come apart."

        Row
            Button "step"
                on click
                    add 1 to visits
            Button "run to the end"
                on click
                    set visits to 8
            Button "reset"
                on click
                    set visits to 0

        Row
            Text "visits requested: "
            Text visits

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "depth first"
            Row
                Text "visited: "
                Text depthOrder, weight is "bold"
            Row
                Text "stack, front to back: "
                Text depthQueue, color is "grey"
            each node in nodes
                if depth.seen contains node
                    Text node, color is "green"
                otherwise
                    Text node, color is "grey"

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "breadth first"
            Row
                Text "visited: "
                Text breadthOrder, weight is "bold"
            Row
                Text "queue, front to back: "
                Text breadthQueue, color is "grey"
            each node in nodes
                if breadth.seen contains node
                    Text node, color is "green"
                otherwise
                    Text node, color is "grey"

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "the answers"
            Row
                Text "depth first: "
                Text depthAnswer
            Row
                Text "breadth first: "
                Text breadthAnswer
